{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The single core result domain for every performed evaluation is {satisfied, falsified, indeterminate, out_of_frame, invalid_basis}. Respectively these mean: the declared predicate holds on the exact basis; a declared counterexample defeats it; admissible evidence cannot decide it; an undeclared material relation or capability is required; or the subject or basis cannot support the evaluation. Constraints, judgments, frame evaluations, stops, work steps and effects use this core directly or through refinements. Non-performance is absence of a result, not a sixth value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":801,
          "quote": "| `satisfied` | the declared predicate holds on the exact basis |\n| `falsified` | a declared counterexample defeats it |\n| `indeterminate` | the available admissible evidence cannot decide it |\n| `out_of_frame` | the evaluation requires an undeclared material relation or capability |\n| `invalid_basis` | the subject or basis cannot support the evaluation |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line":797
        }
      ],
      "key": "core_result_algebra",
      "label": "Core result algebra"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A domain refinement specifies its question, mapping predicate and finer outcomes, each mapped to exactly one core value. Its defining member, profile or adopting Product retains ownership of domain meaning. A further refinement may distinguish causes through child values naming a parent and cause without changing the parent's core value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":875,
          "quote": "A further\nrefinement, by a member, profile, project or adopting Product, may split a\nrefined value by cause. The parent-value rule governs every such split:\neach child value names its parent value and its cause and keeps its\nparent's core value; a child that needs a different core value needs a\ndifferent parent value, which only the owner of the parent declares; and\nno child takes `satisfied` from a parent that is not `satisfied`.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line":869
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_refinement",
      "label": "Result refinement"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Availability asks whether a step produced a candidate; assessment asks whether that candidate meets its declared criteria; acceptance asks whether its decision owner accepts it at the owner's boundary. These are separate results with separate predicates. An evaluation result and a step stop state likewise answer different questions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":891,
          "quote": "Availability, assessment and acceptance are separate results, each with its\nown predicate: that a step produced a candidate; that the candidate meets\nits declared criteria; and that the\n[decision owner](ACTIVATION.md#roles-and-delegation) accepts it at its\nboundary. A step having produced a candidate never maps to the candidate's\nfitness or acceptance, and acceptance is never inferred from a successful\nstep",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line":885
        }
      ],
      "key": "construction_result_predicates",
      "label": "Availability, assessment and acceptance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Conjunction combines component results into a result for a composite predicate, including a multi-evaluation decision or composed steps' outer contract. Its distinct inputs are applicable closed results with predicate and domain outcome; planned assessments lacking an applicable closed result; and required coverage R minus the coverage C admitted by planned assessments. A declared decision rule owns the permitted policy choices.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1030,
          "quote": "1. the closed results returned, each a core value with its predicate and\n   domain outcome;\n2. the planned assessments that have no closed result applicable to the\n   composite: evaluations the composite requires that were not performed,\n   or whose result is absent, stale, bound to another subject or basis, or\n   incapable of establishing its claim;\n3. the required coverage that no planned assessment addresses: for a\n   required obligation set R and the coverage C that the planned\n   assessments admit, the uncovered coverage R minus C.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FConjunction%20of%20results",
          "start_line":1022
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_conjunction",
      "label": "Conjunction of results"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Family F3 traces result and combination rules to commitments, instance premises, consequences, owning rules, added choices and Pillars. Its instance premises are declaration of an evaluation's predicate, subject and basis; combining several evaluations, steps or members into a decision; and need for finer outcomes. Its consumer index locates applications across ACTIVATION, REFERENCE_FRAME, AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS, EVALUATION, RELEASE, CONFORMANCE, ASSURANCE, DESIGN_MODULE, SPEC_METHOD, TICKETS, EXECUTIVE_BASELINE, UX, WORLD_MODEL, TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE, ODD and PRODUCT_DEFINITION. These are source-stated applications, not independently verified applications.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":951,
          "quote": "Instance premises, which the commitments do not supply:\n(i) an evaluation declares its predicate, subject and basis; (ii) results\nof several evaluations, steps or members are combined into one decision;\n(iii) members, profiles and adopting Products need outcomes finer than the\ncore.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line":947
        },
        {
          "end_line":984,
          "quote": "Consumers of these rows, one list per row, each consumer linked to its own\nlocus:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line":983
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_foundation_and_consumers",
      "label": "Result foundation-to-law trace and consumers"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The governed situation supplies the applicable instance premises: construction basis and material assumptions Γ; declared case domain Ω with material inputs, behaviour and environment; and a checker with declared observations and admitted evaluation relation."
      ],
      "definition": "The source presents assurance relations as the common owner connecting construction to assertions, observations and evidence, covering preservation, assertion, provenance, quantified assurance, reuse and formal representation. Its foundation-to-law table distinguishes commitments from situation-specific premises and separates consequences from choices and their owners. The notation expresses proof obligations rather than their discharge.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1149,
          "quote": "Its instance premises are: (a) a construction\nwith the governing basis bound at intake under ACTIVATION §1 and the\nmaterial assumptions, together written Γ here; (b) a declared domain Ω of cases, each with the inputs,\nbehaviour and environment material to an obligation; (c) a checker with\ndeclared observations and an admitted evaluation relation. They are\nconditions of the situations the family governs, not commitments.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssurance%20relations",
          "start_line":1144
        }
      ],
      "key": "assurance_relations",
      "label": "Assurance relations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A construction binds starting state A and required outcome Z, including material effects and trace properties, under governing basis and assumptions Γ. At boundary i, X_i is the state or trace domain, F_i ⊆ X_i × X_(i+1) is the transition relation, and P_i is the property retained or established. A preservation claim states universally that P_i(x) and F_i(x,x') imply P_(i+1)(x'). Algorithmic, informational and computational accounts respectively describe the required operation, retained meaning and distinctions, and realisation of work and observations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1192,
          "quote": "For a sequence of transformations, let `X_i` be the state or trace domain\nat boundary i, `F_i ⊆ X_i × X_(i+1)` the declared transition relation, and\n`P_i` the property to retain or establish there. A claimed preservation\nstep has the form:\n\n```text\nFor all x, x':  P_i(x) and F_i(x, x')  implies  P_(i+1)(x').",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FPreservation%20through%20construction",
          "start_line":1186
        },
        {
          "end_line":1223,
          "quote": "The algorithmic account states the required operation, the informational\naccount the meaning and distinctions retained, and the computational\nbinding how work and observations are realised",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FPreservation%20through%20construction",
          "start_line":1221
        }
      ],
      "key": "construction_preservation",
      "label": "Preservation through construction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "For selected obligation r, Q_r = interpret(r, Γ, Ω) is its required predicate over declared cases. An assertion targets q_t and distinguishes full obligation from narrower claim. Q_r ⇒ q_t makes q_t necessary; q_t ⇒ Q_r makes it sufficient; and (q_1 ∧ ... ∧ q_n) ⇒ Q_r makes the stated conjunction sufficient. All directions are conditional on Γ and Ω.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1244,
          "quote": "Q_r implies q_t                   : q_t is a necessary condition of Q_r.\nq_t implies Q_r                   : establishing q_t is sufficient for Q_r.\n(q_1 and ... and q_n) implies Q_r : the stated conjunction is sufficient.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line":1242
        }
      ],
      "key": "assertion_interpretation",
      "label": "Obligation interpretation and assertion directions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A checker's observation map is π: Ω → O and its admitted evaluation relation is T ⊆ O × R, with R the core result algebra. A deterministic checker is a functional special case; human or probabilistic checkers may admit multiple conclusions on identical observations. sat and fal denote judgments about q_t, not execution states. Soundness universally requires (π(s),sat) ∈ T ⇒ q_t(s) and (π(s),fal) ∈ T ⇒ ¬q_t(s) for every s ∈ Ω. Always deciding correctly from π alone requires equal observations to imply equal q_t truth values for every pair of admissible cases.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1257,
          "quote": "Let `π: Ω → O` describe the observations available to a checker and\n`T ⊆ O × R` its admitted evaluation relation, where R is the core result\nalgebra of Results above. A deterministic checker is a functional special\ncase. A human or probabilistic checker can admit different conclusions on\nthe same observations; a universal soundness claim covers all admitted\nconclusions, not a favourable selected run.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line":1252
        },
        {
          "end_line":1290,
          "quote": "For all s, s' in Ω:\n  π(s) = π(s')  implies  q_t(s) = q_t(s').",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line":1289
        }
      ],
      "key": "observation_checker",
      "label": "Observation and admitted checker relation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Construction and assurance may derive separately from a common obligation: requirement-derived assertions check applicable requirement conditions in the realisation, while refinement-derived assertions check its detailed obligations. Assertion provenance comprises the owning obligation, interpretation, assumptions, derivation and shared dependencies. Execution provenance comprises the actual checker, exact subject, environment, inputs, observations and result. Authentication binds a returned assessment to producer, subject, basis and evidence but establishes neither provenance. Mutual assurance compares independently justified constructions or assertions within stated scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1323,
          "quote": "An expected result or acceptance predicate has assertion provenance: the\nowning obligation, its interpretation, assumptions, derivation and shared\ndependencies, which give it meaning. An execution has execution\nprovenance: the actual checker, exact subject, applicable environment,\ninputs, observations and result. Both are needed for the claims they\nsupport; execution records alone cannot establish that the expected\nanswer follows from the requirement. Authentication of a returned\nassessment, which binds a result to its producer, subject, basis and\nevidence ([EVALUATION §3](EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment)), is a\nthird thing and establishes neither provenance (P6, P3).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FParallel%20derivation%20and%20mutual%20assurance",
          "start_line":1314
        }
      ],
      "key": "provenance_and_mutual_assurance",
      "label": "Parallel derivation, provenance and mutual assurance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A numerical assurance claim is made under a declared model and evidence."
      ],
      "definition": "Quantified assurance models a specified violation event V and evidence event E under Γ. Its Bayesian posterior is P(E|V,Γ)P(V|Γ) divided by [P(E|V,Γ)P(V|Γ) + P(E|not V,Γ)P(not V|Γ)]. For check misses M_i, the joint miss probability P(M_1 and ... and M_n|V,Γ) factors as the product over i of P(M_i|V,Γ,M_1,...,M_(i-1)). These are model-dependent probability relations, distinct from predicate truth or whole-product correctness.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1375,
          "quote": "P(V | E, Γ) =\n  P(E | V, Γ) P(V | Γ)\n  / [P(E | V, Γ) P(V | Γ) + P(E | not V, Γ) P(not V | Γ)].",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FQuantified%20assurance",
          "start_line":1373
        },
        {
          "end_line":1389,
          "quote": "P(M_1 and ... and M_n | V, Γ)\n  = product over i of P(M_i | V, Γ, M_1, ..., M_(i-1)).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FQuantified%20assurance",
          "start_line":1388
        }
      ],
      "key": "quantified_assurance",
      "label": "Quantified assurance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Assertion justification explains why a check expresses an obligation; evidence validity identifies dependencies affecting behaviour, conclusion or applicability, including implementation, libraries, data, instruments, environments and assumptions. For material change Δ, including discovery of an invalid assumption, impact cover I(Δ) conservatively covers changed facts and affected dependents; D*(t) covers transitive dependencies material to assertion, execution and evidence use. Candidate selection is S_Δ = {t : D*(t) intersects I(Δ)}. The new evidence account is retain_applicable(E_old,new_subject,new_basis) union E_reassessed.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1425,
          "quote": "Let Δ be a change to material facts or dependencies, including a newly\ndiscovered invalid assumption. Let `I(Δ)`, the impact cover, conservatively cover the changed\nfacts and their affected dependents. For each check t, let `D*(t)` cover\nthe transitive dependencies material to its assertion, execution and use\nof evidence. A candidate selection is:\n\n```text\nS_Δ = { t : D*(t) intersects I(Δ) }.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20reuse",
          "start_line":1418
        },
        {
          "end_line":1447,
          "quote": "E_new = retain_applicable(E_old, new_subject, new_basis) union E_reassessed.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20reuse",
          "start_line":1447
        }
      ],
      "key": "change_impact_and_evidence_reuse",
      "label": "Change impact and evidence reuse"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The subject's candidate a_c interpretation is a derived representation of material obligations and dependencies under its owning Product's constraint-surface fidelity rule and acquired views. It distinguishes stated formula, discharged mathematical argument, mechanically checked structure, externally assessed predicate and execution evidence. Source semantics, formal fidelity and actual use have separate evidence. This describes the subject's representation role without assigning such standing to this interpretation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1504,
          "quote": "It distinguishes five kinds of support for a statement: a\nstated formula, a discharged mathematical argument, mechanically checked\nstructure, an externally assessed predicate, and evidence from execution.\nAn external predicate retains the source, scope and evidence required to\nestablish it; an arbitrary truth label does not discharge the obligation.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FFormal%20representation",
          "start_line":1500
        }
      ],
      "key": "formal_representation",
      "label": "Formal representation and support kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The source carries four hypotheses for falsification rather than as law: categorical equivalence of 𝒜 and ℐ under some reformulation; universal algorithmic capture of meaning; the statistical Markov boundary with candidate status routed to WORLD_MODEL §4 and predecessor provenance; and traversal composition laws of any runtime.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1536,
          "quote": "Not law; carried for falsification: categorical equivalence of 𝒜 and ℐ under\nsome reformulation; universal algorithmic capture of meaning; the statistical\nMarkov boundary (candidate status under\n[WORLD_MODEL §4](WORLD_MODEL.md#epistemic-status); provenance\n[WORLD_MODEL_METHOD](stdo://releases/v2.5.1-rc.2/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#epistemic-status-of-the-construct));\ntraversal composition laws of any runtime.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F5.%20Hypotheses",
          "start_line":1531
        }
      ],
      "key": "foundational_hypotheses",
      "label": "Hypotheses carried for falsification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The source-disposition register reports predecessor-to-corpus conservation and ownership. AC position/provenance/scope route to AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §1; signature and five records to §§2–3; AC-001 through AC-014 to §4, with FOUNDATION objects and generic result, latitude, reference and conservation rules shared rather than redefined; AC traversal/functor laws and AC-015/016 to §5; interpretation/models and AC-017–019 to §6; carrier boundaries and AC-020 to §7; exact identity to §8 with its record a release artefact; application/import/exclusion/conformance to §§9–10. The AC Fundamental Laws heading is reported not applicable because it contains no claim text (20 bytes). RF definitions route to FOUNDATIONS §2; RF laws, declarations, functions, coverage and closure to REFERENCE_FRAME; engagement and qualification to ACTIVATION and EVALUATION. RF #definitions is a 15-byte claim-free heading whose children are separately disposed. These are reported source dispositions, not conservation findings made here.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1555,
          "quote": "| AC Exact Calculus Identity | conserved; the identity law is restated in AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §8; the record instance is a release artefact issued under RELEASE and AC-020 at this corpus's own release |\n| AC Application Boundary, External Imports And Specializations, Exclusions, Conformance Obligations | conserved; restated in AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §9 and §10 |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F6.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":1554
        },
        {
          "end_line":1590,
          "quote": "| #definitions | not applicable: heading with no claim text (15 bytes); its children are disposed individually | §2 |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F6.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":1590
        }
      ],
      "key": "source_disposition_register",
      "label": "Reported source dispositions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The glossary is a non-deciding locator index from indexed spellings to current-corpus clauses declaring contexts and owning concepts. It realises the glossary named by PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5 and adds no law. Its clauses cite requiring principles P0–P6. An rc.2 #anchor route targets the predecessor GLOSSARY_GUIDE.md solely as provenance, without requiring that cut for application.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":10,
          "quote": "Guide. A non-deciding locator index from indexed spellings to the clauses\nof this corpus that declare their bounded contexts and own their concepts.\nIt realises the glossary that\n[PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5](PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#5-bounded-context-semantic-resolution)\nnames and adds nothing to that law. Every clause names the principle that\nrequires it (P0 to P6, FOUNDATIONS §3). A route written `rc.2 #anchor` is\ninto `stdo://releases/v2.5.1-rc.2/standards/GLOSSARY_GUIDE.md` and is\nprovenance only; nothing here depends on that cut to be applied.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY",
          "start_line":3
        }
      ],
      "key": "glossary_locator_index",
      "label": "Glossary locator index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An ordinary index row records indexed spelling, bounded-context identity declared by the cited owner, and exact owning concept clause. The alternate §17 row records a multiply-used label, a locator for its occurrence and an owner. The owner plus selected complete basis identifies a candidate meaning; the occurrence supplies governed scope. Imported concepts cite their defining section and import declaration or stated owner relation. Concepts outside every context declared by their member use that member as source context, indexed in §16 without declaring a new context.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":30,
          "quote": "Each row records an indexed spelling, the bounded-context identity\ndeclared by the cited owning clause, and the exact owning concept clause.\n§17 uses a second row form for a label that several clauses use: the\nlabel, a locator naming where that use occurs, and the owning clause. The\nlocator only says where to look; it defines nothing and declares no\ncontext, which the owning clause supplies;\n§2 states how a row records a concept that a context holds by import, and\na concept whose member declares no bounded context for it.\nThe owning clause together with the selected basis identifies the candidate meaning;\nthe occurrence being resolved still supplies its own governed scope",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F1.%20Index%20contract",
          "start_line":20
        },
        {
          "end_line":64,
          "quote": "A concept defined outside every bounded context its member declares has\nthat member as its source context\n([FOUNDATIONS §2 Imports between members](FOUNDATIONS.md#imports-between-members)).\n§16 indexes those concepts with the member in place of a bounded-context\nidentity. Such a row declares no context, and a context holds one of those\nconcepts only by its own import",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F2.%20Locator%20law",
          "start_line":59
        }
      ],
      "key": "glossary_index_row",
      "label": "Glossary row and locator roles"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Locator set for urn:stdo:bounded-context:method-identity, declared by SPEC_METHOD.md#method-identity. Both STDO and the O pillar symbol route to that owner. STDO has no relation clause; O additionally points to ODD.md#1-position. These routes do not supply the concepts' definitions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
